 would be a pawn grab but risks opening lines for White.\n\nReason: The best move is likely **P: c7-c5**. This move immediately challenges the center and opens lines for Black's queen and dark-squared bishop. It also exerts pressure on d4 and e3, potentially forcing White to react. It's a standard aggressive opening move that helps Black equalize and gain activity. N: g8-f6 is also good, but playing c5 first is more flexible and fights for the center right away. B: e7-f6 is too early as the bishop can be easily chased away with g3 or h3.\n\nAnswer: P: c7-c5"}
